project typhed_project_data {
  database_type: 'postgresql'
  note: '''
  # TyPhed Project Data

  The account and entitlement database for the TyPhed ecosystem, owned by the
  `typhed/typhed.project.data` service. It is the only server-side datastore in
  the ecosystem, because `typhed.com` is a static export on GitHub Pages with no
  runtime of its own.

  ## Schema Layout

  Four schemas in one database, one Alembic migration tree, cross-schema foreign
  keys all anchored on `identity.clerk_users_mw.clerk_id`.

  | Schema | Holds | Written By |
  |:-------|:------|:-----------|
  | `identity` | Mirrored Clerk identity, login history, webhook ledger | Signed webhook only |
  | `profile` | The ecosystem-wide user profile | The authenticated user |
  | `catalog` | Products, grants and the entitlement audit trail | Payment webhook or admin |

  ## Naming Conventions

  - `_mw` suffix marks a master table, `_tx` an append-only transactional one.
  - Constraints follow `pk_<table>`, `uq_<table>_<column>`,
    `fk_<table>_<column>_<referred_table>`, `ck_<table>_<constraint>`,
    `ix_<column_label>`.
  - Enumerations are stored as `varchar(32)` guarded by a `check` constraint,
    never as a native PostgreSQL `enum`. Adding a payment provider is then
    ordinary DDL rather than an `alter type`.

  ## Who Writes What

  Ownership is the organising principle of the whole design. A browser may author
  profile answers and nothing else. Identity moves only when a Svix-signed Clerk
  webhook says so, and an entitlement moves only on a signed payment callback or
  an explicit administrative action.

  ## Privacy Constraints

  TyPhed never tracks or shares user data, which has concrete consequences here:

  - `identity.login_events_tx` stores a timestamp, a Clerk session id and the
    origin subdomain. **No IP address, no user-agent, no geolocation.** The
    origin names one of our own products, not a person.
  - No payment instrument is stored anywhere. `catalog.user_products_mw` keeps a
    provider customer id and a status, which is enough to reconcile and nothing
    worth stealing.
  - Deletion is soft for identity and hard for content. Every user-owned row is
    removed outright and the identity row is retained as a tombstone with every
    personal column nulled, so the id can never be reused.
  '''
}

table identity.clerk_users_mw {
  clerk_id                varchar(255)  [pk]                    // pk_clerk_users_mw
  email_id                varchar(320)  [null, unique]          // ix_identity_clerk_users_mw_email_id · unique
  username                varchar(255)  [null, unique]          // ix_identity_clerk_users_mw_username · unique
  first_name              varchar(255)  [null]
  last_name               varchar(255)  [null]
  full_name               varchar(512)  [null]
  image_url               varchar(2048) [null]
  primary_email_verified  boolean       [null]
  provisioned_by          varchar(32)   [not null]              // ck_clerk_users_mw_provisioned_by · webhook | jit
  clerk_updated_at        timestamptz   [null]
  deleted_at              timestamptz   [null]                  // ix_identity_clerk_users_mw_deleted_at
  synced_at               timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]
  created_at              timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]
  updated_at              timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]

  note: '''
  ## Clerk Identity Anchor (`mw` = Master)

  The mirrored Clerk identity and the anchor every other schema references. This
  table is the single source of truth for whether a user exists.

  ### Relationships

  - **Referenced by** `identity.login_events_tx.clerk_id`,
    `profile.user_profiles_mw.clerk_id`, `catalog.user_products_mw.clerk_id` and
    `catalog.user_product_events_tx.clerk_id`, all
    (`on update no action`, `on delete cascade`).
  - Deliberately **not** referenced by `identity.webhook_events_tx`, which must
    outlive a purge so that retries of a user's own deletion stay idempotent.

  ### Check Constraints

  - `ck_clerk_users_mw_provisioned_by` - `webhook` when a Clerk event created the
    row, `jit` when the first authenticated request did.

  ### Nullability

  Every column except `clerk_id` is nullable, and that is deliberate. Clerk
  treats usernames and names as optional, and a row provisioned just-in-time from
  a session token knows only the `sub` claim, so a `not null` anywhere else turns
  an ordinary sign-up into a failed webhook delivery.

  ### The Two Guards

  - `clerk_updated_at` is the payload's own `updated_at`, never the delivery
    timestamp. Svix delivers at least once and may reorder, so an upsert carrying
    an older value than the stored one is discarded rather than applied.
  - `deleted_at` is the tombstone. Every upsert carries
    `where deleted_at is null`, so a `user.updated` arriving after a
    `user.deleted` cannot resurrect the account. A returning user is issued a
    fresh Clerk id, so the tombstone never blocks a legitimate return.

  ### Backfill

  A row whose `email_id` is null is a skeleton awaiting enrichment, filled in by
  the next `user.updated` or by a one-time backfill against the Clerk API.
  '''
}

table identity.login_events_tx {
  id                bigint        [pk, increment]  // pk_login_events_tx · generated by default as identity
  clerk_id          varchar(255)  [not null, ref: > identity.clerk_users_mw.clerk_id]  // fk_login_events_tx_clerk_id_clerk_users_mw · on update no action · on delete cascade
  clerk_session_id  varchar(255)  [not null, unique]  // uq_login_events_tx_clerk_session_id
  origin            varchar(255)  [null]
  occurred_at       timestamptz   [not null]

  indexes {
    (clerk_id, occurred_at)  [name: 'ix_login_events_tx_clerk_id_occurred_at']
  }

  note: '''
  ## Login History (`tx` = Transaction)

  One row per completed sign-in, written from the Clerk `session.created`
  webhook. This table is the privacy position of the whole service made concrete.

  ### Relationships

  - **Parents:** `identity.clerk_users_mw` via
    `fk_login_events_tx_clerk_id_clerk_users_mw`.

  ### What Is Deliberately Absent

  A row records **when** a sign-in happened, **which Clerk session** it was, and
  **which of our own subdomains** it landed on. There is no IP address, no
  user-agent, no device fingerprint and no geolocation, because the brand claim
  is that TyPhed never tracks users and a claim that holds only until it is
  inconvenient is not a claim. The `origin` value identifies a product, not a
  person. A test asserts this table has exactly five columns.

  ### Composite Indexes

  - `ix_login_events_tx_clerk_id_occurred_at`: (`clerk_id`, `occurred_at`) -
    supports keyset pagination of one user's history. The single-column index on
    `clerk_id` is deliberately omitted, since this one already covers it as its
    leftmost column.

  ### Deduplication

  `clerk_session_id` is unique so a redelivered event is discarded with
  `on conflict do nothing`, rather than by a read that could race a concurrent
  delivery.

  ### Origin Stamping

  Clerk's `session.created` payload does not carry the origin, so the row is
  written with `origin` null and the first authenticated call from that session
  fills it in from the token's `azp` claim.
  '''
}

table identity.webhook_events_tx {
  id              bigint        [pk, increment]  // pk_webhook_events_tx · generated by default as identity
  source          varchar(32)   [not null]       // ck_webhook_events_tx_source · clerk | stripe | razorpay | paddle | lemon_squeezy
  external_id     varchar(255)  [not null]
  event_type      varchar(128)  [not null]       // ix_identity_webhook_events_tx_event_type
  subject_id      varchar(255)  [null]           // ix_identity_webhook_events_tx_subject_id
  payload_digest  varchar(64)   [null]
  attempts        integer       [not null, default: 0]
  received_at     timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]
  processed_at    timestamptz   [null]

  indexes {
    (source, external_id)  [unique, name: 'uq_webhook_events_tx_delivery']
  }

  note: '''
  ## Webhook Delivery Ledger (`tx` = Transaction)

  Provider-agnostic record of every inbound webhook delivery, used to make
  processing idempotent. Clerk uses it today and the payment provider will use it
  unchanged.

  ### Relationships

  - **No foreign key to the user, by design.** The ledger has to outlive a purge,
    or a retry of a user's own deletion would fail against the very rows that
    deletion removed.

  ### Composite Unique Constraints

  - `uq_webhook_events_tx_delivery`: (`source`, `external_id`) - the idempotency
    key. Two providers may mint the same event id without colliding, and one
    provider's event cannot be processed twice.

  ### Check Constraints

  - `ck_webhook_events_tx_source` - which upstream delivered the event.

  ### Claim-Then-Process

  Receipt and completion are separate columns on purpose. Claiming a delivery
  inserts the row and increments `attempts` but leaves `processed_at` null, so a
  crash between the claim and the write leaves the event unprocessed and the
  provider's retry picks it up. Marking it processed is the last thing the
  handler does. Reversing that order turns any mid-handler failure into permanent
  silent data loss, because the retry is then discarded as a duplicate.

  ### Naming

  `subject_id` rather than `clerk_object_id`: the table serves payment providers
  too, and a Clerk-specific column name on a Stripe row would be a lie. It is a
  plain index and **not** unique - one subject emits many events, and
  `user.created` and `user.updated` carry the same object id.
  '''
}

table profile.user_profiles_mw {
  clerk_id                 varchar(255)  [pk, ref: > identity.clerk_users_mw.clerk_id]  // pk_user_profiles_mw · fk_user_profiles_mw_clerk_id_clerk_users_mw · on update no action · on delete cascade
  display_name             varchar(120)  [null]
  headline                 varchar(200)  [null]
  country_code             varchar(2)    [null]
  attributes               jsonb         [not null, default: `'{}'::jsonb`]
  completion_percent       integer       [not null, default: 0]  // ck_user_profiles_mw_completion_percent_range
  profile_completed_at     timestamptz   [null]
  onboarding_dismissed_at  timestamptz   [null]
  profile_schema_version   integer       [not null, default: 1]
  created_at               timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]
  updated_at               timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]

  note: '''
  ## User Profile (`mw` = Master)

  The one table in this database a browser may write, by authenticated `patch`.
  One row per user, keyed on the identity anchor itself.

  ### Relationships

  - **Parents:** `identity.clerk_users_mw` via
    `fk_user_profiles_mw_clerk_id_clerk_users_mw`.

  ### Check Constraints

  - `ck_user_profiles_mw_completion_percent_range` - `completion_percent` stays
    between 0 and 100.

  ### Hybrid Storage

  The field contract lives in `brand/profile.json`, where each field declares
  `"storage": "column"` or `"storage": "attribute"`. A promoted field maps to a
  real column here; everything else lives in `attributes`. Adding an attribute
  field is a commit to the contract with no migration at all. A field earns
  promotion to a column when it is filtered on, sorted on, read by another
  service, or needs a database constraint.

  The API rejects any key the contract does not declare with a `422`, which is
  what keeps `attributes` from becoming a dumping ground.

  ### Why Completion Is Stored And Not Computed

  `profile_completed_at` is stamped once, when the weighted score first crosses
  the contract's threshold, and never cleared. This is not redundancy. If
  completion were derived from the contract on every read, adding a single field
  to `brand/profile.json` would retroactively un-complete every account in the
  system and re-open the onboarding wizard for the entire user base at once - a
  JSON commit with no code change, quietly becoming a support incident.

  `profile_schema_version` is the deliberate escape hatch for when you genuinely
  do want to re-prompt everyone. The server decides whether onboarding is
  required; the client never recomputes the threshold.
  '''
}

table catalog.products_mw {
  product_id   varchar(64)   [pk]  // pk_products_mw
  name         varchar(200)  [not null]
  subdomain    varchar(255)  [not null]
  description  text          [null]
  is_paid      boolean       [not null, default: false]
  is_active    boolean       [not null, default: true]
  created_at   timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]
  updated_at   timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]

  note: '''
  ## Product Catalogue (`mw` = Master)

  The products in the ecosystem, mirrored from `brand/products.json` so the
  database can hold foreign keys against a catalogue that is authored in the
  brand contract.

  ### Relationships

  - **Referenced by** `catalog.user_products_mw.product_id` via
    `fk_user_products_mw_product_id_products_mw` and
    `catalog.user_product_events_tx.product_id` via
    `fk_user_product_events_tx_product_id_products_mw`, both
    (`on update no action`, `on delete no action`).

  ### Seeded Values

  Seeded by migration from `shared/documents/brand/products.json` with an
  upserting insert, so re-running the seed after a contract change converges
  rather than colliding on the primary key. Currently `blog` and `trading`.

  ### Important Distinction

  `is_paid` says whether the product **ever** charges, not whether a given user
  is paying. That question is answered by `access_tier` and `status` on
  `catalog.user_products_mw`.

  A product is deactivated rather than deleted, so a historical grant and its
  audit trail keep pointing at something that still exists.
  '''
}

table catalog.user_products_mw {
  clerk_id                  varchar(255)  [pk, ref: > identity.clerk_users_mw.clerk_id]  // pk_user_products_mw · fk_user_products_mw_clerk_id_clerk_users_mw · on update no action · on delete cascade
  product_id                varchar(64)   [pk, ref: > catalog.products_mw.product_id]  // pk_user_products_mw · fk_user_products_mw_product_id_products_mw · on update no action · on delete no action
  access_tier               varchar(32)   [not null]  // ck_user_products_mw_access_tier · free | trial | paid
  status                    varchar(32)   [not null]  // ck_user_products_mw_status · active | past_due | cancelled | expired
  source                    varchar(32)   [not null]  // ck_user_products_mw_source · self_signup | payment_webhook | admin_grant
  registered_at             timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]
  started_at                timestamptz   [null]
  expires_at                timestamptz   [null]  // ix_catalog_user_products_mw_expires_at
  cancel_at_period_end      boolean       [not null, default: false]
  provider                  varchar(32)   [null]
  provider_customer_id      varchar(255)  [null]  // ix_catalog_user_products_mw_provider_customer_id
  provider_subscription_id  varchar(255)  [null]  // ix_catalog_user_products_mw_provider_subscription_id
  created_at                timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]
  updated_at                timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]

  note: '''
  ## Product Grant (`mw` = Master)

  One user's standing on one product. Never writable by a browser: a grant
  arrives from a signed payment webhook or an explicit administrative action.

  ### Relationships

  - **Parents:** `identity.clerk_users_mw` and `catalog.products_mw`.

  ### Check Constraints

  - `ck_user_products_mw_access_tier` - what kind of access the user is meant to
    have.
  - `ck_user_products_mw_status` - whether that access is currently in force.
  - `ck_user_products_mw_source` - how the grant arrived. `self_signup` is the
    only value a browser can cause, and only ever for a free tier.

  ### Registration Is Not Entitlement

  These are different questions and they live in different columns. A user can
  register interest in a paid product without paying
  (`access_tier = 'free'`, `status = 'active'`), and a lapsed subscriber keeps
  their registration while losing entitlement
  (`access_tier = 'paid'`, `status = 'past_due'`). Collapsing the two into one
  boolean makes "has an account with us" and "is currently paying"
  indistinguishable, and makes a lapsed subscriber look like a stranger.

  Entitlement is granted only when the status is `active`, the expiry has not
  passed, and - on a paid product - the tier is something other than `free`.

  `past_due` is deliberately distinct from `expired`: the first is a payment that
  failed and may still succeed, the second is access that has ended. Collapsing
  them loses the window in which a user can be asked to fix their card rather
  than being told to sign up again.

  ### No Payment Instruments

  Only provider identifiers are stored. Card numbers, expiries and CVVs belong to
  the payment provider, which already holds them. A test asserts structurally
  that no column name here resembles a payment instrument.

  ### Upgrades

  The key is `(clerk_id, product_id)`, so an upgrade moves this row rather than
  adding another. `started_at` records when the relationship began and survives a
  plan change. The history of every move lives in
  `catalog.user_product_events_tx`.
  '''
}

table catalog.user_product_events_tx {
  id           bigint        [pk, increment]  // pk_user_product_events_tx · generated by default as identity
  clerk_id     varchar(255)  [not null, ref: > identity.clerk_users_mw.clerk_id]  // fk_user_product_events_tx_clerk_id_clerk_users_mw · on update no action · on delete cascade
  product_id   varchar(64)   [not null, ref: > catalog.products_mw.product_id]  // fk_user_product_events_tx_product_id_products_mw · on update no action · on delete no action
  from_tier    varchar(32)   [null]      // ck_user_product_events_tx_from_tier · free | trial | paid
  to_tier      varchar(32)   [not null]  // ck_user_product_events_tx_to_tier · free | trial | paid
  from_status  varchar(32)   [null]      // ck_user_product_events_tx_from_status · active | past_due | cancelled | expired
  to_status    varchar(32)   [not null]  // ck_user_product_events_tx_to_status · active | past_due | cancelled | expired
  reason       varchar(255)  [null]
  actor        varchar(128)  [not null]
  occurred_at  timestamptz   [not null, default: now()]

  note: '''
  ## Entitlement Audit Trail (`tx` = Transaction)

  Append-only record of every grant, upgrade, downgrade and revocation. A row is
  never updated or deleted except when the account itself is deleted.

  ### Relationships

  - **Parents:** `identity.clerk_users_mw` and `catalog.products_mw`.

  ### Why This Table Decides The Stack

  The grant and its audit row are written **in one transaction**. This is the
  reason the service runs on asyncpg against real PostgreSQL rather than an HTTP
  database driver with no interactive transactions. An entitlement that moved
  with no trace is unauditable, and a trace of a move that never happened is
  worse, because it will be believed. Both become possible the moment the two
  writes can succeed independently.

  ### Actor

  Who caused the change: a provider name, an operator identifier, or `self` when
  a user registered themselves for a free tier.

  ### Deletion

  These rows are purged when the account is deleted, along with the grants. They
  are keyed to a person, and a deletion request that left a record of what that
  person paid for would not be a deletion.
  '''
}

tablegroup identity_owned_by_clerk {
  identity.clerk_users_mw
  identity.login_events_tx
}

tablegroup delivery_ledger {
  identity.webhook_events_tx
}

tablegroup owned_by_the_user {
  profile.user_profiles_mw
}

tablegroup products_and_money {
  catalog.products_mw
  catalog.user_products_mw
  catalog.user_product_events_tx
}
